  <title>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Working With Informality - ?Re-imagining? Khayelitsha Project</narrative>
  </title>
  <description>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Since its establishment as an apartheid &#x2018;dormitory town&#x2019; in 1984, Khayelitsha has mushroomed into a sprawling settlement south-east of Cape Town.  With a population now estimated at 1m people, the settlement exhibits high levels of violence, crime and poverty &#x2013; presenting everyone with huge social and economic challenges.  Most official interventions to date have tended to focus on providing physical infrastructure and basic services, with little attention given to more participatory processes that would empower local citizens and lead to improved social cohesion.  In the past 18 months escalating violence (vigilantism) and service delivery protests have increasingly slowed government&#x2019;s input, creating a vicious circle of inaction and deeper frustration still, especially in the informal areas.    A new way of working is clearly needed, which will build trust between local communities, NGOs, and government. This project aims to respond to that need by working with residents to improve housing and basic facilities, taking advantage of government funding to encourage more community-driven improvements. A programme of action on security of tenure is part of this.  The project will improve livelihoods and develop the skills of young unemployed people too.    In addition to addressing some of Khayelitsha&#x2019;s socio-economic and housing problems, the project will also bring out the rich history of those who live in the area, helping to restore a sense of pride in the township as a vibrant &#x2018;hub&#x2019;, increasing a sense of belonging to the thriving metropolis of Cape Town.  This grant will fund the six-month inception phase which will enable DAG to carry out exploratory/planning work with partners and community members.</narrative>
  </description>
